      "source": [
        "# Welcome to the Fruit Fly Sex Classification Pipeline! 🪰\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook is a complete, end-to-end pipeline for classifying fruit flies as either `female` or `male`.\n",
        "\n",
        "We care a lot about making sure our AI doesn't \"cheat\" by memorizing backgrounds or specific camera setups. To ensure our model is actually learning what a male or female fly looks like, this notebook focuses on:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Preventing data leaks**: Making sure the same fly doesn't accidentally end up in both the training and testing sets.\n",
        "- **Fair evaluations**: We test the model on completely untouched data.\n",
        "- **Comparing different images**: We test if the model performs better on tightly cropped images versus full images.\n",
        "- **Stress testing**: We mess with the images (blurring, changing brightness) to see if the model breaks.\n",
        "- **Looking under the hood**: We use tools to see *where* the model is looking to make its decisions.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Our Golden Rule for Testing\n",
        "We divide our data into strict categories:\n",
        "1. **Training Data**: Used to teach the model.\n",
        "2. **Validation Data**: Used to tweak settings and pick the best model.\n",
        "3. **The Untouched Test Set**: We lock this away until the very end to get a 100% honest score.\n",
        "4. **Challenge Copies**: Modified versions (like blurry or dark images) to test robustness.\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook is designed to be easy to follow in case somebody wanted to improve upon the model in the future. Good Luck!"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "id": "73622d28",
